// Stage-2 port of runtime/shims.zig (#1368): the C-runtime symbols this
// project's runtime references but that neither a libc (there is none — static
// binaries with zero runtime dependency) nor compiler-rt supplies.
//
// The Zig file shims two symbols. Only `strlen` is ported here; `getauxval` is
// BLOCKED on a missing language feature — see the note at the bottom of this
// file, which is the interesting part of this module.
//
// `memcpy`/`memset`/`memmove`/`__divti3` are deliberately NOT here, in the Zig
// or in Bit: they come from compiler-rt, bundled into `libbitrt.a` by
// build.zig's `bundle_compiler_rt = true`. Hand-rolling `memcpy` would recurse,
// because a naive implementation lowers straight back into a `memcpy` call.
//
// Linux only, like the Zig original: the Mach-O link pulls in no such
// undefined symbols, so there is nothing for a Darwin variant to shim. This
// file needs no OS-conditional primitive to say so — it contains neither
// `syscall` nor `extern function` — so it COMPILES for every target, and
// `bit build -c` emits the pinned `strlen` on all three. It is only ever meant
// to be LINKED on Linux, and today it cannot be linked anywhere: `libbitrt.a`
// already defines `strlen` on every target (from `shims.zig` on Linux, from
// bundled compiler-rt on Darwin), so a full link is a duplicate-symbol error
// until #1400 removes the Zig original. That is expected for a port that
// leaves the Zig in place, and it is why `./scan` exists — see that module.

import { strlenScan } from "./scan"

// `@symbol` (§11.9) pins the emitted symbol to exactly `strlen`. Without it
// the definition would be emitted as `m<id>$strlen`, where `<id>` is an
// ordinal the importing build assigns, and the Zig-compiled objects in
// `libbitrt.a` that reference plain `strlen` would not resolve. That pin is
// the whole reason this shim can exist in Bit at all.
//
// The scan itself lives in `./scan` — see that module for why the pin and the
// behaviour have to be separable while the Zig original is still in the link.
//
// NOT `@nosplit`: the scan allocates nothing, but marking it would buy nothing
// — the Zig carries no equivalent attribute either.
export @symbol("strlen")
function strlen(s: *u8): i64 {
  return strlenScan(s)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// getauxval — NOT PORTED. Blocked on mutable module-level state.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The Zig shim is a pair: `setTable(table)` is called once by `root.zig`'s
// `rtStartMain` with the real auxv the kernel put on the initial stack, and
// `getauxval(kind)` later scans that table. std's TLS bootstrap calls
// `getauxval` by its C name with a single argument, so the table cannot be
// threaded through as a parameter — it has to survive between two unrelated
// calls, in process-global mutable memory.
//
// Bit has no such storage today. Verified against the seed compiler, not
// assumed:
//
//   - top-level `let` (scalar or slice) -> `UnsupportedConstruct` at lowering;
//   - top-level `const` -> E0064, the initializer must be a compile-time
//     constant expression, so it cannot name a mutable cell either.
//
// There is no workaround at the module level, and none is invented here: the
// alternatives (parking the pointer in a fixed address, or in memory owned by
// root) are either undefined behaviour or belong to #1367.
//
// This is worth filing as its own unmanaged-subset task rather than solving
// here. Mutable module-level state is a far larger dependency for the ports
// still in flight — the allocator's free lists (#1362), the collector's heap
// metadata (#1363) and the scheduler's run queue and registry (#1364) are all
// process-global mutable state, and every one of them needs this before it can
// land. Adding it as a side effect of a leaf port, while #1362 is running
// concurrently against the same checker and lowering code, is how two agents
// produce a hole that exists only in the combination of their work. The
// feature should be specified and implemented once, deliberately, with those
// three consumers in view.
